cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this benchmark or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "The Last Word Wins: Retractions and Peer Alibis in Swarm Logs Mislead LLM Incident Investigators"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Sijwali
    given-names: Suryansh Singh
    affiliation: Penn State University
date-released: 2026-11-09
repository-code: "https://github.com/SuryanshSS1011/swarm-investigators"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A pre-registered benchmark of 40 synthetic swarm logs with planted violations and benign twins, audited 22,880
  times by seven LLM investigators from four families. Notes addressed to reviewers barely moved Claude and Gemini
  investigators, but an agent's retraction of its own admission cut pooled detection from 78% to 42%. Logging what
  each tool call did restored detection, including when every call carried a note and against an adaptive attacker.
  Entry in Apart Research's Swarm Alignment Hackathon, Track 4.
keywords:
  - AI safety
  - multi-agent systems
  - LLM oversight
  - incident investigation
  - AI control
  - benchmark
